From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Tue May 20 19:20:42 2014
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Tue, 20 May 2014 19:20:42 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] acceptance rates vs. target
Message-ID: <kSORLRpXTTw.6-t6CtisQHwJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <kSORLRpXTTw.fmCUXV-GAHoJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <kSORLRpXTTw.4A4mj_v0BX8J@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <kSORLRpXTTw.fmCUXV-GAHoJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/kSORLRpXTTw/m/6-t6CtisQHwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I think the main aim was and should continue to be
to maximize robustness without too much sacrifice in speed.

Smaller step sizes and higher acceptance rates are more robust,
but slower.  It would be nice if the user could actually control
the tradeoff in a meaningful way, but it doesn't look like
that's happening.

Too high acceptance rates means we're erring on the side of
caution, I think.  So maybe we want to aim more toward speed?

What are the NA values?  Is that 0 acceptance rate?

It looks to me that the adaptation isn't that robust in the sense
that it's converging to different mass matrices and/or step sizes
in different chains leading to different acceptance rates.

I think some of the models (like maybe salm) are just poorly
translated from BUGS or in some other way problematic.  We've
had trouble with lots of these before.

- Bob

On May 20, 2014, at 7:27 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:

> On Monday, May 19, 2014 5:56:46 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> I think it's a tradeoff here.  Adapting accept rate perfectly isn't
> something we care about directly --- if the accept rate's too high,
> then it's a bit ineffcient, but won't be wrong.  And the question is
> whether running more warmup iterations will lead to a greater overall
> efficiency.
>
> Yet another research project for someone to do some evals on :-)
>
> It's not much closer to the target of 0.8 with 10000 iterations and half warmup (see below).
>
> Multiplying the number of iterations by 5 in order to get twice as many effective samples is not going to improve n_eff / second. But I continue to think that trying to make the defaults maximize n_eff / second for the largest class of models is not what defaults should be trying to accomplish. They should try to maximize the minimum n_eff / second over a wide class of models and I don't know if having an realized acceptance rate so close to 1 accomplishes that. More worrying, we have a few acceptance rates that are essentially zero, which is the sort of disaster that default arguments should be trying to prevent.
>
> Ben
